Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Orion Scrapes The Third Rail
- Story & Context: On a freezing Tuesday aboard the night express, Mara Kovač accidentally dropped a pocket synth directly into the cello’s soundboard just as Big Dave Static launched into a verse about spotting the constellation Orion through a cracked skylight at the depot. The resulting feedback-drenched jazz-funk jam caught the ear of forty exhausted maintenance workers who refused to exit at their stop. "The sky is a dark roof with small pinholes, but this iron car is where the blood rolls," Dave growled over a brutal, grinding cello riff that sounded like a freight train locking its brakes. That raw recording, complete with background track-announcements, became the foundational blueprint for the entire scene.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album art featuring a grainy rooftop view of a dark city skyline under a stark star constellation, overlaid with a rusted metal train track running directly into the night sky, high-contrast black-and-white print with stark orange typography.

Track 2: Eyepiece Glare at Sector 4
- Story & Context: Written after Vance Vega spent six hours on a cold Brooklyn roof adjusting a brass telescope only to have his view blocked by steam vents, this track zeroes in on the frustrating beauty of urban astronomy. During a live performance in a moving train car, Vega hit a cluster of minor 9th Rhodes chords while Mara dragged a brass telescope tube across her cello strings instead of a bow. "You pay five dollars for a peek at Saturn's ring, but the smoke from the laundry tells you another thing," Static whispered into a clipping PA mic. The track captures the quiet humiliation of looking for infinity while standing in puddle-soaked boots.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic shot of a heavy brass telescope pointed out of an open train door toward a foggy streetlamp, light reflecting off a scratched cello body resting against industrial metal walls, moody blue and amber tones.

Track 3: Kovač, Put The Bow Down
- Story & Context: The legendary closing session of the EP recorded live in the train yard after the line shut down for the night due to track repair. The story goes that Mara refused to stop playing even after her horsehair bow snapped completely in half, prompting Dave Static to yell the title track name before grabbing a scrap of iron rebar from the tracks and handing it to her. "Put the wood away, bring the iron out today, we got twenty miles of track left to translate," Static barked as Mara struck the cello strings with the metal rod, producing a brutal, resonant clang that mixed perfectly with pocket-synth sub-bass. It solidified the myth of the scene: turning broken transportation tools into unyielding, soul-funk poetry.
